import type {ResearchPost} from './fleet-data';

export const november18ResearchBatch:readonly ResearchPost[]=[{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-forecast-demand-when-a-client-changes-its-billing-cycle","title":"How should a contact center forecast demand when a client changes its billing cycle?","excerpt":"A re-mapping method for a moved billing cycle, with offset history, calendar checks, and a forecast error test before staffing is locked.","published":"2026-11-18","sourceDate":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","image":"/daily18/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-forecast-demand-when-a-client-changes-its-billing-cycle.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks how a contact center should forecast demand after a client changes its billing cycle. The population is one client's inbound queue, and the unit is a weekly arrival forecast for the first full cycle after the change. The question is whether the old history still supports a staffing plan or must be re-mapped. The analysis covers calendar effects, contact reasons, and error measurement, not the client's pricing decision. Record the old cycle, the new cycle, and the change date before rebuilding the forecast."},{"heading":"What the evidence supports","body":"Workforce-management practice calls for a forecast built from historical arrivals, known calendar events, and confirmed business changes. Federal data-quality guidance asks analysts to document the source, test reliability, and state limitations. Neither source supplies a formula for a moved billing date. The operating inference is to re-map old arrivals onto the new calendar and then test the result against the first live cycle. A forecast that ignores the move will place the peak on the wrong week even when the total volume is right."},{"heading":"Map the old cycle to the new one","body":"Start by plotting the old billing date, the statement date, and the payment due date on one calendar. Mark the days when volume rose in the past and the reason customers gave for calling. Then move each date to the new cycle and keep the distance between the statement and the due date. A shift of three days is not a simple shift of every call, because weekends, paydays, and holidays also move. Write the mapping in a table so a reviewer can check each date."},{"heading":"Rebuild the contact history","body":"Re-cut at least two years of arrivals so each contact sits at the same offset from the billing date. Use the offset rather than the calendar week, because the old and new cycles do not line up. Tag the contacts that mention a statement, a payment, or a due date. Contacts with no billing link stay on the normal seasonal pattern. Label the source report and the extraction date on every table."},{"heading":"Separate cycle effects from seasonality","body":"A moved billing date can land on top of an existing seasonal peak, which makes the change look larger than it is. Build two views: one with the billing offset and one with the ordinary weekday and month pattern. Compare the residuals so the seasonal part is not attributed to the cycle. If the new date lands near a holiday, model that week separately rather than smoothing it away."},{"heading":"Check the promotion calendar","body":"Ask the client for every promotion, price change, and reminder campaign that will run in the new cycle. A message that tells customers the bill is ready will pull contacts forward by a day or two. Put the send dates on the same calendar as the forecast and mark the expected lift or delay. A forecast built only from billing dates will miss the campaign effect."},{"heading":"Revisit the contact reason mix","body":"A new cycle can change why customers call, not only when they call. A shorter grace period may raise payment calls, while a clearer statement may lower balance questions. Classify the last cycle by reason and compare the mix with the same offset a year earlier. Use the difference to adjust the skills you schedule, not only the headcount. Ask for the send time, not only the send date."},{"heading":"Test the channel mix","body":"Voice, chat, email, and self-service do not move together when a bill changes. A statement error may produce a long voice call, while a due-date question may be answered in chat. Forecast each channel that has a service target and keep the total visible beside it. A single blended forecast can hide a voice peak behind a drop in email."},{"heading":"Model the weekly arrival pattern","body":"Use half-hour arrivals for the first two weeks after the new statement date. The daily total can look stable while the morning and evening peaks move by an hour. Check the pattern against the client's payment posting times and the reminder send schedule. Staffing built from a weekly total will be late for the first peak and early for the last."},{"heading":"Set the staffing lead time","body":"Write down how many days it takes to add a shift, move a break, or approve overtime. Compare that lead time with the number of days between the forecast freeze and the first peak. If the lead time is longer, the plan needs a trigger that acts on the earliest reliable signal. A forecast that arrives after the schedule is locked cannot change the queue."},{"heading":"Plan the first billing cycle","body":"Treat the first cycle as a monitored transition rather than a normal month. Set a weekly review, a named analyst, and a rule for when to add a small staffing move. Keep a control group of intervals that will not change so the effect is visible. Write the review dates before the cycle starts. Name the person who can approve the extra shift."},{"heading":"Measure the forecast error","body":"Compare the forecast with actual arrivals at the same interval and offset. Report the error in contacts and in agents, because a small percentage can still mean several people. Separate the error that came from the cycle from the error that came from the normal weekday pattern. A single average error hides the peak that caused the service failure. Use the same interval definition as the schedule."},{"heading":"Give the client a variance note","body":"Send a short note after each review that states the forecast, the actual, the difference, and the likely cause. Name the change the team will make and the date it will be tested. Avoid a note that only explains the past without a next step. The client should be able to see whether the new cycle is behaving as planned."},{"heading":"Protect the queue during the transition","body":"Keep a buffer in the first two weeks for a billing error that reaches many customers at once. Define the trigger for opening a dedicated queue or adding a self-service message. Do not wait for the abandon rate to rise before using the buffer. The buffer is a planned cost of the transition, not a sign that the forecast failed."},{"heading":"Review the billing exceptions","body":"List the cases that did not follow the new cycle, such as a prorated bill or a payment arrangement. Count them separately so they do not distort the main pattern. If the exceptions are numerous, the forecast may need a second model for that population. Ask the client which exceptions will continue after the transition. Ask whether the exception will repeat next cycle."},{"heading":"Document the assumptions","body":"Write the assumptions in the forecast file: the cycle dates, the campaign schedule, the contact reasons, and the expected lift. Note which assumptions came from the client and which came from the analyst. A later reviewer should be able to see why the numbers changed. Undocumented assumptions turn a forecast into an opinion. Date the file so a later change is easy to trace."},{"heading":"Test the model with a backcast","body":"Apply the new method to an older period where the cycle did not change. Compare the backcast with the actual arrivals and with the old method. If the new method is worse in a stable period, the re-mapping may be too complex. Keep the simpler method where it performs better and document the choice. Write the comparison beside the chosen method."},{"heading":"Limits of the evidence","body":"This brief cannot predict the exact volume for a specific client, because the answer depends on the customer base, the statement design, and the payment terms. A re-mapped history can support a plan, but it cannot remove the uncertainty of a first cycle. The sample may miss a one-time campaign or a system change. Use the result to set a range and a review, not to promise a precise number."},{"heading":"Manager review questions","body":"Managers should ask which dates moved, which contact reasons depend on the bill, and what campaign will run in the first cycle. They should also ask how the forecast error will be measured and who can approve a staffing move. The remaining question is which assumption will be tested first. Record the answers with the period and the source data."},{"heading":"Conclusion","body":"A moved billing cycle needs a re-mapped history, a campaign calendar, and a forecast error test before the schedule is locked. The total volume can look right while the peak lands on the wrong day. Build the offset view, compare it with the seasonal pattern, and keep a buffer for the first cycle. The forecast is useful when it changes a staffing decision in time to protect the queue."}],"sources":[{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","organization":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","date":"2026","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm","note":"Employment, wage, training, and outlook figures for customer service representatives."},{"name":"ISO 18295-1:2017 Customer contact centres","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2017","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/64739.html","note":"Requirements for customer contact centre processes, people, and performance."},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","organization":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","date":"2020","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g","note":"A practical framework for documenting data sources, testing reliability, and stating limitations."},{"name":"Fact Sheet #64: Call Centers under the Fair Labor Standards Act","organization":"U.S. Department of Labor, Wage and Hour Division","date":"2008","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/64-flsa-call-centers","note":"Hours-worked, rest-period, and recordkeeping rules for call center employees."}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Re-cut history by offset from the billing date instead of by calendar week.","Put the promotion and reminder send dates on the same calendar as the forecast.","Report forecast error in contacts and in agents for each interval.","Keep a named buffer and a weekly review for the first full cycle."],"faqs":[{"question":"Should the old forecast be discarded after a cycle change?","answer":"No. Keep it as a comparison and re-cut the history by billing offset. The old pattern still carries information about which request types follow a statement."},{"question":"How long should the transition be monitored?","answer":"At least the first full cycle and one normal month after it. A single cycle can be distorted by a campaign or a one-time error."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-what-should-a-workforce-plan-assume-about-shrinkage-in-the-first-ninety-days","callcenteroutsourced-how-should-an-occupancy-target-mislead-a-queue-review"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the queue design","body":"Bring the current queue rules, the shift handoff, and one week of cases so the review can test a real promise."}},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-what-should-a-workforce-plan-assume-about-shrinkage-in-the-first-ninety-days","title":"What should a workforce plan assume about shrinkage in the first ninety days?","excerpt":"A first-ninety-day shrinkage model that separates planned time, new-hire attrition, and coaching load before the schedule is approved.","published":"2026-11-18","sourceDate":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","image":"/daily18/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-what-should-a-workforce-plan-assume-about-shrinkage-in-the-first-ninety-days.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks what a workforce plan should assume about shrinkage during an agent's first ninety days. The population is a new-hire class in one outsourced queue, and the unit is a scheduled hour that does not produce available phone time. The question is whether the standard shrinkage figure fits a class that is still training and ramping. The analysis covers categories, measurement, and review, not the legal classification of any absence. Record the class size, the start date, and the schedule model before comparing figures."},{"heading":"What the evidence supports","body":"Federal wage-and-hour guidance requires accurate records of hours worked and payment for compensable time. Contact-center standards call for forecasting, scheduling, and performance measurement, but they do not supply a first-ninety-day shrinkage rate. The operating inference is to build the assumption from the class's own schedule and observed events. A mature-floor percentage applied to a new class will usually understate the time that is not available for calls."},{"heading":"Define the shrinkage categories","body":"Write the categories before measuring anything: vacation, holiday, sick leave, training, nesting, coaching, meetings, system outage, and unexcused absence. Give each category an owner and a code in the timekeeping system. Separate the time that is planned in advance from the time that appears without notice. A single shrinkage number hides the difference and makes the plan hard to defend."},{"heading":"Measure the baseline","body":"Pull the last two or three classes and calculate shrinkage by category for each week of the first ninety days. Use scheduled hours as the denominator and record the source of the hours. Do not compare a class that started in a holiday month with one that started in a quiet month. Show the range across classes, not only the average."},{"heading":"Separate planned from unplanned","body":"Planned time includes training, nesting, coaching, and approved leave, and it can be built into the schedule. Unplanned time includes sick leave, outages, and no-shows, and it needs a buffer. A plan that treats all shrinkage as one number cannot decide whether to add a shift or hold a reserve. Report the two groups separately to the client. Show the reserve in hours and in people."},{"heading":"New-hire attrition","body":"A class can lose people during training and in the first weeks on the queue. Model the loss by week and keep the reason categories honest. Do not assume the full class will reach the floor, because the schedule and the client forecast both depend on the number who do. Review the assumption with human resources before the plan is approved."},{"heading":"Training and nesting time","body":"Training and nesting are productive investments, but they are not available phone time. Put the hours in the shrinkage model and in the coaching plan so the same time is not counted twice. A new agent who is in nesting for part of a shift should be scheduled for the remaining share, not for a full queue load. Record the transition date for each agent."},{"heading":"Coaching and meetings","body":"A new class needs more coaching than a tenured team, and the coaching hours belong in the plan. Include team meetings, one-to-one reviews, and quality calibration in the planned category. If the coach is also scheduled for calls, the coaching time reduces queue capacity twice. Name the coverage for the coach's own queue time. A coach who is pulled into calls cannot deliver the plan."},{"heading":"Absence patterns in the first ninety days","body":"Review absence by week and by reason for the previous classes. A spike in the first month may reflect onboarding logistics rather than a pattern that will continue. A spike after the first solo week may reflect the shock of full volume. Use the pattern to set a reserve, not to predict an individual's behavior. Ask human resources which reasons need a different code."},{"heading":"Schedule adherence during onboarding","body":"New agents have more schedule changes than tenured agents because training and nesting move. Measure adherence against the schedule in force and code the approved changes. A low adherence score in the first month may be a scheduling artifact. Fix the coding before using the number in a performance review. Publish the schedule version used in the review. Record the approved change in the same system the adherence report reads."},{"heading":"The supervisor span","body":"A supervisor with a new class has less time for the tenured team, and that effect belongs in the plan. Count the coaching hours and the escalation time the class will consume. If the span is already at its limit, add a coach rather than stretching the supervisor. A plan that ignores the span will look affordable and fail in the first month."},{"heading":"Overtime and its limits","body":"Overtime can cover a gap, but it is not a shrinkage assumption. Repeated overtime raises fatigue and absence, which raises shrinkage in the next period. Use overtime as a short buffer with a named approval and a review date. Do not build a permanent schedule on the assumption that people will always accept extra hours. Track the decline in acceptance before it becomes a crisis."},{"heading":"Build the plan with a range","body":"Present the shrinkage assumption as a low, middle, and high case for the first ninety days. Tie each case to a specific event, such as a larger class or a holiday start. Show the headcount and the service effect for each case. A single number invites a false sense of precision. Name the event that would move the plan to the high case."},{"heading":"Test the assumption","body":"Compare the planned shrinkage with the actual for the first four weeks. Break the gap down by category and by week so the cause is visible. If the gap is in coaching, fix the coaching plan. If it is in absence, review the reserve and the support available to new agents. Set the next check before the four weeks end."},{"heading":"Report the gap to the client","body":"Give the client a short table of planned versus actual shrinkage by category. State what changed and what the team will do about it. Do not present a single percentage without the mix. The client should see whether the plan needs a new hire or a schedule change. Keep the same categories in every report. Use the same categories in the client note and the internal review."},{"heading":"Review at thirty, sixty, and ninety days","body":"Set three review points and write the questions for each. At thirty days, check training and nesting. At sixty days, check the first solo weeks and the coaching load. At ninety days, check whether the class has reached the tenured assumption. A review that happens only at the end cannot correct the plan. Write the owner for each review question."},{"heading":"Document the assumptions","body":"Keep the class list, the schedule, the category definitions, and the source of the hours with the plan. Note which assumptions came from the client and which came from the operations team. A later reviewer should be able to see why the reserve was set at a particular level. Undocumented assumptions make the plan impossible to defend. Attach the class roster and the approved schedule."},{"heading":"Limits of the evidence","body":"This brief cannot supply a shrinkage rate for a specific site, because the answer depends on the labor market, the schedule, the client's training, and the local law. Historical classes can inform a range, but they cannot predict a new class with certainty. The sample may be small. Use the result to set a range and a review, not to promise an exact headcount. Keep the ninety-day review separate from the annual performance cycle."},{"heading":"Manager review questions","body":"Managers should ask which categories are planned and which are not, how the class size is expected to change, and who owns the coaching hours. They should also ask what the trigger is for adding a coach or a shift. The remaining question is which category has the widest gap. Record the answers with the period and the class data."},{"heading":"Conclusion","body":"A first-ninety-day plan needs a shrinkage assumption built by category, not borrowed from the tenured floor. Training, nesting, coaching, attrition, and absence behave differently in a new class. Present a range, measure the gap every four weeks, and keep the review dates. The plan is sound when the client can see what the reserve is for and when it will be tested."}],"sources":[{"name":"Fact Sheet #64: Call Centers under the Fair Labor Standards Act","organization":"U.S. Department of Labor, Wage and Hour Division","date":"2008","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/64-flsa-call-centers","note":"Hours-worked, rest-period, and recordkeeping rules for call center employees."},{"name":"Fair Labor Standards Act","organization":"U.S. Department of Labor, Wage and Hour Division","date":"2026","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/flsa","note":"Federal minimum wage, overtime, hours-worked, and recordkeeping obligations."},{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","organization":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","date":"2026","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm","note":"Employment, wage, training, and outlook figures for customer service representatives."},{"name":"ISO 18295-1:2017 Customer contact centres","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2017","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/64739.html","note":"Requirements for customer contact centre processes, people, and performance."}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Build first-ninety-day shrinkage from categories, not from the tenured-floor average.","Separate planned training, nesting, and coaching from unplanned absence and outages.","Model new-hire attrition by week and keep the class size in the staffing plan.","Review the gap at thirty, sixty, and ninety days and adjust the reserve."],"faqs":[{"question":"Should training time count as shrinkage?","answer":"Yes, for capacity planning. It is planned and productive, but it is not available phone time, so it belongs in the model with its own category."},{"question":"Can one shrinkage number cover every queue?","answer":"No. The mix depends on the schedule, the class, and the local labor market. Use a range and review it against actual hours."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-forecast-demand-when-a-client-changes-its-billing-cycle","callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-verify-a-caller-before-an-account-change"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the queue design","body":"Bring the current queue rules, the shift handoff, and one week of cases so the review can test a real promise."}},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-verify-a-caller-before-an-account-change","title":"How should a contact center verify a caller before an account change?","excerpt":"A risk-based verification design for account changes, with factor choice, failed-check handling, and evidence that survives a review.","published":"2026-11-18","sourceDate":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","image":"/daily18/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-verify-a-caller-before-an-account-change.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks how a contact center should verify a caller before making an account change. The population is an inbound queue that can update an address, a payment method, or a contact preference. The unit is a single change request, and the question is whether the verification matches the risk of that change. The analysis covers factor choice, failure handling, and evidence, not the client's legal identity proofing program. Record the request types and the systems that can make each change."},{"heading":"What the evidence supports","body":"Federal digital-identity guidance describes authentication assurance levels and warns that knowledge-based questions are weak when the answers are widely available. Cybersecurity guidance recommends multifactor authentication and resistance to social engineering. Privacy guidance asks businesses to protect customer information and honor the commitments they make. The operating inference is to match the verification strength to the risk of the change, not to apply one script to every caller."},{"heading":"Classify the request by risk","body":"List the changes the queue can make and rank them by the harm a wrong change would cause. A contact preference is low risk, while a bank account or a shipping address for a high-value order is high risk. A password reset may be higher still because it opens the whole account. Give each tier a verification standard and a named approver for exceptions."},{"heading":"Choose factors that fit the risk","body":"For a low-risk change, a verified account number and a callback to the number on file may be enough. For a high-risk change, add a second factor such as a one-time code to the registered device or a confirmation through the client's app. Do not treat a date of birth and a postcode as strong factors, because both appear in many data breaches. Write the factor list for each tier."},{"heading":"Do not use knowledge questions alone","body":"A question about a previous address or a pet's name can be answered by someone who has the customer's mail or social profile. Use knowledge questions only as a supplement, never as the only control for a high-risk change. If the client insists on them, record the decision and the risk in the account file. Review the questions each year for how widely the answers are available."},{"heading":"Protect the one-time code","body":"Send the code to the number or device already on file, not to a number the caller provides during the call. Tell the caller what the message will say so a fake message is easier to spot. Never read the code back to the caller or ask them to confirm it in a chat that the agent can see. A one-time code is only a control when it reaches the real account holder."},{"heading":"Handle a failed verification","body":"Write the path for a caller who cannot pass the check. Offer a callback to the number on file, a visit to a verified location, or a review by a specialist. Do not let the agent improvise a workaround under pressure. Record the failure and the alternative so the pattern is visible. Tell the customer how long the alternative will take."},{"heading":"Record the verification event","body":"Log which factors were checked, when, against which contact record, and who approved an exception. Keep the result with the change, not only in the call notes. A later reviewer should be able to see that the standard was met without listening to the whole call. Use the same record for a dispute or a client audit. Restrict edits to the case record after the change is made."},{"heading":"Train agents on social engineering","body":"Teach agents to recognize pressure, urgency, and a caller who knows a lot but cannot pass the factor. Give them a script that pauses the call and escalates without accusing the customer. Practice the script in a short drill, because a real caller will not wait while the agent finds the policy. Reward the agent who stops a suspicious change."},{"heading":"Review a sample of high-risk changes","body":"Pull a weekly sample of high-risk changes and check the factor, the record, and the approval. Look for a pattern where one agent or one shift accepts weaker evidence. Share a good example and a near miss with the team. The review is a control, not a search for someone to blame. Include a caller who is polite and persistent as well as one who is loud."},{"heading":"Set the lockout and retry rules","body":"Decide how many failed attempts are allowed before the account is protected. Make the rule apply to the change, not to the caller's patience. Give the real customer a clear recovery path that does not depend on the agent's judgment. Record the lockout and the recovery in the account history. Keep the sample size and the reviewer in the log."},{"heading":"Keep the process accessible","body":"A verification step that requires a smartphone can exclude a customer who does not own one. Offer an alternative that provides the same assurance, such as a branch visit or a mailed letter. Test the alternative with a real customer scenario. Accessibility is part of the control, not an exception to it. Test the recovery path with a customer who lost the registered phone."},{"heading":"Work with the client's identity team","body":"Ask the client which systems hold the trusted contact data and who can change it. A queue that verifies against a stale record will pass the wrong person. Agree on the fallback when the trusted data is missing or disputed. Put the agreement in the account guide. Record the alternative used so the client can see the coverage. Test the alternative with a customer who has no smartphone and no printer."},{"heading":"Test the process with a drill","body":"Run a drill with a caller who tries to change an address without the registered device. Check whether the agent follows the tier, records the failure, and offers the right alternative. Time the drill and note where the agent had to guess. Fix the guide or the tool before the next live case. Name the person who can correct a stale record."},{"heading":"Report verification failures","body":"Give the client the number of failed checks, the reasons, and the alternative used. Separate a customer who could not pass from a caller who refused. A rising failure count can signal a bad contact record or a new fraud pattern. Report the trend with the sample size. Repeat the drill when a new system changes the factor. Include the time the agent spent finding the rule in the drill note."},{"heading":"Document the decision","body":"Write a short standard that names the tiers, the factors, the exception owner, and the review date. Keep it beside the call scripts so an agent can find it during a call. Version the standard when the client changes a system or a policy. A standard that lives only in training slides will drift. Show the failures beside the volume so the rate is readable."},{"heading":"Review after a breach or a change","body":"A data breach, a new app, or a changed phone number can weaken a factor overnight. Recheck the trusted contact data and the fallback path. Tell the client which requests need a stronger factor during the transition. Review the sample more often until the change is stable. Review the standard with the client's risk owner. Print the step that changed and post it with the shift brief."},{"heading":"Limits of the evidence","body":"This brief cannot certify a verification design for a specific account, because the answer depends on the client's risk, the data it holds, and the law that applies. The guidance describes reasonable practice, not a guarantee against fraud. A determined attacker may still pass a control. Use the standard to raise the cost of a wrong change and to keep evidence of the decision."},{"heading":"Manager review questions","body":"Managers should ask which changes are high risk, which factors are used for each tier, and who can approve an exception. They should also ask how a failed check is recorded and what alternative the customer receives. The remaining question is which factor is weakest in the current data. Record the answers with the request types and the review date."},{"heading":"Conclusion","body":"Verification should match the harm a wrong change would cause, not the length of a script. Use factors that reach the real account holder, record the event, and give the agent a safe path when the check fails. Test the process with a drill and review high-risk changes every week. The control works when the customer can complete it and the record can prove it happened."}],"sources":[{"name":"NIST Special Publication 800-63B: Digital Identity Guidelines","organization":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","date":"2017","url":"https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-3/sp800-63b.html","note":"Authentication and authenticator guidance, including one-time codes and knowledge-based verification limits."},{"name":"Cybersecurity Best Practices","organization":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency","date":"2026","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cybersecurity-best-practices","note":"Practical guidance on passwords, multifactor authentication, and social-engineering resistance."},{"name":"Privacy and Data Security Guidance","organization":"Federal Trade Commission","date":"2026","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/privacy-security","note":"Business guidance on protecting customer information and honoring privacy commitments."},{"name":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","organization":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","date":"2024","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework","note":"Govern, identify, protect, detect, respond, and recover functions used for control ownership."}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Rank every change the queue can make and set a verification tier for each.","Use a one-time code to the contact on file for high-risk changes, never to a number given on the call.","Log the factors, the record, and the approver with the change itself.","Review a weekly sample of high-risk changes and drill the failed-check path."],"faqs":[{"question":"Is a date of birth enough to verify a caller?","answer":"No. It is widely available and often exposed in breaches. Use it only as a supplement to a stronger factor for a high-risk change."},{"question":"What should an agent do when a caller refuses verification?","answer":"Offer the approved alternative and record the refusal. Do not make the change on a promise to verify later."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-what-should-a-workforce-plan-assume-about-shrinkage-in-the-first-ninety-days","callcenteroutsourced-when-does-an-occupancy-target-mislead-a-queue-review"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the queue design","body":"Bring the current queue rules, the shift handoff, and one week of cases so the review can test a real promise."}},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-when-does-an-occupancy-target-mislead-a-queue-review","title":"When does an occupancy target mislead a queue review?","excerpt":"A review of occupancy as a diagnostic, with the denominator, the interval view, and the service measures that keep it honest.","published":"2026-11-18","sourceDate":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","image":"/daily18/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-when-does-an-occupancy-target-mislead-a-queue-review.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks when an occupancy target misleads a contact center queue review. The population is one queue and one review period, and the unit is an interval of scheduled agent time. The question is whether a high or low occupancy number supports the conclusion a manager wants to draw. The analysis covers definitions, denominators, and paired measures, not the design of an incentive plan. Record the queue, the interval, and the source of the hours before comparing targets."},{"heading":"What the evidence supports","body":"Contact-center standards call for forecasting, scheduling, and performance measurement, but they do not set one occupancy target for every queue. Federal wage-and-hour guidance requires accurate records of hours worked and rest periods. The operating inference is to use occupancy as a question about the balance between work and available time, not as a score on its own. A number without the service result and the interval pattern can point to the wrong fix."},{"heading":"Define occupancy precisely","body":"Write the numerator and denominator in plain language. Occupancy is usually the time agents spend handling contacts divided by the time they are available to handle contacts. Decide whether after-call work, hold time, and internal calls count as handling. Apply the same definition to every team in the comparison. A definition that changes between reports makes the trend meaningless. Keep a worked example beside the definition."},{"heading":"Check the denominator","body":"A high occupancy can come from a small denominator rather than a large workload. Check whether the available time includes meetings, coaching, and system downtime. If those hours are excluded, the queue will look busier than it is. Reconcile the scheduled hours with the timekeeping record before drawing a conclusion. Ask for the raw hours when the report only shows a percentage."},{"heading":"Separate productive and available time","body":"An agent can be busy with after-call work and unavailable for the next call. That is productive time, but it is not the same as talk time. Show the components beside the occupancy figure so the reader can see where the time went. A queue that is busy with wrap work may need a process fix, not more agents. Use the same category names in every interval report."},{"heading":"See what occupancy hides","body":"A high occupancy can hide a long queue because the agents are working as fast as they can. A low occupancy can hide a skill mismatch because the right agents are idle while a specialty queue waits. Occupancy says how full the agents are, not whether the customer is being served. Pair it with the wait time and the abandon rate before acting."},{"heading":"Compare intervals, not averages","body":"A monthly average can look healthy while the morning peak is overloaded and the afternoon is overstaffed. Plot occupancy by interval for a representative week and mark the service level in the same chart. The pattern often shows a scheduling problem that the average conceals. Use the interval view when deciding whether to move a break or add a shift."},{"heading":"Pair occupancy with service level","body":"A target that raises occupancy without a service target can push agents to rush calls or skip after-call work. A service target without occupancy can hide idle capacity that the client is paying for. Show both, plus the abandon rate and the repeat contact rate. The review should discuss the tradeoff, not one number in isolation. Choose the target pair before the review, not after."},{"heading":"Watch the multi-channel effect","body":"An agent handling chat and voice at the same time can show high occupancy while one channel waits. The systems may count concurrent contacts differently, which distorts the ratio. Define how concurrency is treated and keep the channel results separate. A blended figure can make a broken channel look busy. Test the concurrency rule with a sample of agents. Ask the platform vendor how concurrency is counted in the occupancy export."},{"heading":"Review after-call work","body":"After-call work is part of the handling time, but it can also be a sign of a slow system or a missing template. Measure the wrap time beside the occupancy and the handle time. If wrap time is long, the fix may be a form or a knowledge article, not more staff. Reducing wrap time can lower occupancy while improving service."},{"heading":"Check the shrinkage assumption","body":"Occupancy uses available time, and available time depends on shrinkage. If the plan assumes less shrinkage than the queue actually experiences, the occupancy target will be wrong from the start. Compare the planned and actual shrinkage for the same period. A gap there can explain a service failure without any change in agent effort. Name the assumption in the report so the reader can check it."},{"heading":"Test a small staffing change","body":"Before rewriting a schedule, move one break or add one agent in a single interval. Measure the occupancy, the wait, and the abandon rate for that interval and a comparable one. A small test is cheaper than a full schedule change based on a monthly average. Record the result and the date. Keep the change for at least two weeks before judging it."},{"heading":"Avoid a target that rewards burnout","body":"A target that pushes occupancy toward the limit leaves no room for coaching, breaks, or a system problem. It can also reward agents who skip rest periods or avoid difficult calls. Set the target with the service result and the legal rest rules in view. A queue that meets an occupancy goal by burning out the team is not healthy."},{"heading":"Report the range","body":"Give the client the occupancy range across intervals, not only the average. Mark the intervals that failed the service target and the ones that had idle capacity. Show the sample and the period. A range makes the tradeoff visible and gives the client a fair basis for a decision. Note the intervals that had too few calls to judge. Keep the range in the client report, not only the peak figure."},{"heading":"Use occupancy to ask a question","body":"Treat a high or low number as the start of a review. Ask which interval, which channel, and which request type produced it. Read a sample of calls and check the after-call work. The answer should lead to a specific change, such as a schedule move, a template, or a routing rule. Write the answer in one sentence with the evidence beside it."},{"heading":"Document the measure","body":"Write the definition, the source, the interval, and the treatment of exceptions in the report. Note any change to the system or the schedule during the period. A later reader should be able to reproduce the number. An undocumented change makes a comparison unsafe. Attach the export and the date it was pulled. Name the system and the export used in the footnote."},{"heading":"Review the target with the client","body":"Ask the client what decision the occupancy target is meant to support. If the goal is cost control, pair it with the service level. If the goal is service, pair it with the wait and the abandon rate. A target without a decision is a number that invites argument. Review the pair of targets at the next quarterly meeting. Ask the client which decision the target is meant to support this quarter."},{"heading":"Limits of the evidence","body":"This brief cannot set the right occupancy for a specific queue, because the answer depends on the arrival pattern, the service target, the channel mix, and the cost model. The measure can show where the time went, but it cannot prove that an agent worked well. Use the result to frame a test, not to settle a performance dispute. State the period and the queue in the conclusion."},{"heading":"Manager review questions","body":"Managers should ask which denominator was used, which intervals failed, and what the service level did in the same period. They should also ask whether after-call work or a system problem explains the pattern. The remaining question is which small change will be tested first. Record the answers with the interval data. Keep the answers with the interval report. Use the same definition when the target is reviewed next time."},{"heading":"Conclusion","body":"Occupancy misleads when it is read alone, averaged over a month, or built on a denominator nobody checked. Use it with the service level, the interval pattern, and the after-call work. Test a small schedule change before rewriting the plan. The measure is useful when it leads to a specific question about the queue, not when it becomes the goal."}],"sources":[{"name":"ISO 18295-1:2017 Customer contact centres","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2017","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/64739.html","note":"Requirements for customer contact centre processes, people, and performance."},{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","organization":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","date":"2026","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm","note":"Employment, wage, training, and outlook figures for customer service representatives."},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","organization":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","date":"2020","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g","note":"A practical framework for documenting data sources, testing reliability, and stating limitations."},{"name":"Fact Sheet #64: Call Centers under the Fair Labor Standards Act","organization":"U.S. Department of Labor, Wage and Hour Division","date":"2008","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/64-flsa-call-centers","note":"Hours-worked, rest-period, and recordkeeping rules for call center employees."}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Write the occupancy numerator and denominator and apply them to every team.","Check the available-time denominator for meetings, coaching, and system downtime.","Read occupancy by interval with the service level, not as a monthly average.","Test one break or shift move before changing the whole schedule."],"faqs":[{"question":"Is high occupancy always good?","answer":"No. It can mean the queue is understaffed and agents have no time for coaching, breaks, or a system problem. Pair it with the service result."},{"question":"Should after-call work count as occupancy?","answer":"Usually yes, as handling time. Show it separately so a long wrap time points to a process fix rather than a staffing fix."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-verify-a-caller-before-an-account-change","callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-document-a-complaint-that-crosses-two-vendors"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the queue design","body":"Bring the current queue rules, the shift handoff, and one week of cases so the review can test a real promise."}},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-document-a-complaint-that-crosses-two-vendors","title":"How should a contact center document a complaint that crosses two vendors?","excerpt":"A cross-vendor complaint record with one timeline, one owner, a privacy boundary, and a response clock both vendors can follow.","published":"2026-11-18","sourceDate":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","image":"/daily18/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-document-a-complaint-that-crosses-two-vendors.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks how a contact center should document a complaint that crosses two vendors. The population is one customer case that touches the client, the contact center, and a third-party platform or fulfillment partner. The unit is the complaint record and the handoffs it contains. The question is whether the record gives one owner and one timeline across the vendors. The analysis covers evidence, privacy, and review, not the contract dispute between the companies. Record the vendors, the systems, and the dates before writing the timeline."},{"heading":"What the evidence supports","body":"Privacy guidance asks businesses to protect customer information and honor the commitments they make. Federal data-quality guidance calls for documenting sources, testing reliability, and stating limitations. Contact-center standards call for a defined complaint process and clear ownership. The operating inference is to keep one case record that names the owner at each stage and shares only the information each vendor needs. A separate record per vendor produces three versions of the same complaint."},{"heading":"Name the vendors in the record","body":"List every organization that touched the case, including the client, the queue, the platform, and the delivery partner. Add the system and the contact name for each handoff. A vendor that appears only in an email chain is easy to miss when the case is reviewed. Use the same vendor names the client uses in its contracts. Update the list when a new partner joins the case."},{"heading":"Build one timeline","body":"Start the timeline at the customer's first contact and add every action, message, and system event in order. Include the time zone and the source of each entry. Do not overwrite an earlier entry when a later one corrects it; add the correction and the reason. A timeline that can be edited without a trace cannot support a review. Use one time zone for the whole record and note any conversion."},{"heading":"Assign one owner","body":"Give the case one owner at any moment, even when the work sits with a vendor. The owner is responsible for the next update and for chasing the other party. Write the owner's name and the backup in the record. A case with two owners is a case where each side thinks the other is responding. The owner changes only when the record shows an accepted handoff."},{"heading":"Separate the facts from the claim","body":"Record what the customer said, what the systems show, and what each vendor reported as separate fields. Do not merge a vendor's explanation into the customer's account. Mark anything that is unverified. A later reviewer should be able to see where the evidence ends and the interpretation begins. Quote the customer's words rather than a summary. Keep the customer's own words in quotation marks so the summary is not mistaken for a finding."},{"heading":"Set the response clock","body":"Agree with the vendors on a response time for a cross-vendor case and start the clock at the first handoff. Give the customer one update time, not a different promise from each party. Pause the clock only with a recorded reason and an owner. Review the pauses at the weekly case meeting. Put the clock in the shared record, not in one vendor's inbox."},{"heading":"Share only what the customer allowed","body":"Check the client's privacy notice and the customer's consent before sharing account details with a vendor. Send the minimum needed to resolve the case, not the whole record. Use a secure channel rather than a personal email. Record what was shared, with whom, and when. Keep the consent note with the case. Use the client's approved secure file share rather than a chat attachment."},{"heading":"Handle a vendor that does not respond","body":"Write an escalation path for a vendor that misses the clock, including a named contact and a second contact. Keep the customer informed while the internal chase continues. Do not tell the customer that another company is at fault, because that shifts the problem without solving it. Record the missed response and the action taken. Name the second contact before the case is opened."},{"heading":"Keep the customer updated","body":"Send the update at the promised time even when the answer is not ready. State what is known, what is still open, and when the next update will come. Use the customer's preferred channel and language. A missed update is a new complaint, not a neutral delay. Record the message and the channel in the timeline. Keep the update short and put the detail in the timeline."},{"heading":"Record the resolution and the remedy","body":"Write what fixed the issue, who approved the remedy, and what the customer confirmed. Separate a goodwill gesture from a contractual remedy. Keep the evidence with the case. A resolution note that says handled does not tell a later reviewer what changed. Ask the customer to confirm the remedy in their own words. Name the remedy and the date it was applied."},{"heading":"Check the contract and the law","body":"Ask the client which vendor agreement covers the response time and the data sharing. Check whether a regulation sets a notification or response deadline for the case type. Do not promise a remedy the client has not authorized. Record the clause or the rule that supports the decision. Keep the clause reference with the decision. Ask the client's legal contact before promising a remedy that costs money."},{"heading":"Review the complaint for a pattern","body":"Group cross-vendor cases by vendor, request type, and failure point. A repeated delay at one handoff is a process problem, not a run of bad luck. Share the pattern with the client and the vendor without naming the customer. Set one fix and a review date. Compare the pattern across at least two months. Give the vendor the pattern without the customer's identity."},{"heading":"Test the handoff with a drill","body":"Run a practice case through both vendors and time each handoff. Use a scenario that requires a system check on each side. Note where the record was incomplete or the owner was unclear. Fix the template or the contact list before a real case arrives. Time the drill from the first contact to the final confirmation. Keep the drill notes with the case template."},{"heading":"Report the case to the client","body":"Give the client a short summary with the timeline, the owner, the vendor responses, and the outcome. State what is still open and who owns it. Do not send the full customer record unless the client needs it for the review. The summary should let the client answer the customer without another internal search. Keep the customer's personal details out of the summary."},{"heading":"Protect the record from edits","body":"Limit who can change the timeline and log every edit with the editor and the time. Keep the record for the period the client and the law require. A record that can be rewritten after the fact is weak evidence in a dispute. Review the access log when a case is contested. Back up the record on the client's schedule."},{"heading":"Close the loop with the customer","body":"Confirm with the customer that the issue is resolved before closing the case. Ask whether the remedy arrived and whether anything is still missing. Record the answer in the customer's words. A case closed by the vendor's confirmation alone may reopen the next day. Reopen the case if the customer says the remedy did not arrive. Log the reopening and the second resolution in the same record."},{"heading":"Limits of the evidence","body":"This brief cannot decide which vendor is responsible for a specific failure, because that depends on the contract and the facts. A shared record can show what happened and where the handoff waited, but it cannot settle liability. The record may be incomplete if a vendor keeps its own logs. Use the timeline to support a review, not to replace the contract."},{"heading":"Manager review questions","body":"Managers should ask who owned the case at each stage, what each vendor was asked to do, and what the customer was told. They should also ask what data was shared and under which agreement. The remaining question is which handoff failed most often. Record the answers with the case and the vendor list. Name the missing logs in the summary."},{"heading":"Conclusion","body":"A cross-vendor complaint needs one timeline, one owner, a privacy boundary, and a response clock that both parties can see. Separate the facts from the claim and record the remedy with the evidence. Review the pattern and drill the handoff before the next case. The record works when the customer hears one consistent story and the client can see where the delay occurred."}],"sources":[{"name":"Privacy and Data Security Guidance","organization":"Federal Trade Commission","date":"2026","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/privacy-security","note":"Business guidance on protecting customer information and honoring privacy commitments."},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","organization":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","date":"2020","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g","note":"A practical framework for documenting data sources, testing reliability, and stating limitations."},{"name":"ISO 18295-1:2017 Customer contact centres","organization":"International Organization for Standardization","date":"2017","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/64739.html","note":"Requirements for customer contact centre processes, people, and performance."},{"name":"Data Breach Response: A Guide for Business","organization":"Federal Trade Commission","date":"2026","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/data-breach-response-guide-business","note":"Steps for securing affected information, notifying people, and documenting the response."}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Keep one timeline and one named owner for the whole cross-vendor case.","Share only the minimum account data each vendor needs and record what was shared.","Set one customer update time and pause the clock only with a recorded reason.","Review cases by vendor and handoff point, then drill the weakest step."],"faqs":[{"question":"Should each vendor keep its own complaint record?","answer":"A vendor may keep its own notes, but the case needs one shared timeline and one owner. Separate records produce conflicting accounts of the same complaint."},{"question":"Can the queue tell the customer which vendor caused the delay?","answer":"It can explain that another party is working on the case without assigning blame. Name the next step and the update time instead of the responsible company."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-when-does-an-occupancy-target-mislead-a-queue-review","callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-contact-center-forecast-demand-when-a-client-changes-its-billing-cycle"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the queue design","body":"Bring the current queue rules, the shift handoff, and one week of cases so the review can test a real promise."}}] as unknown as readonly ResearchPost[];
